diagnosis_text:
The reported fault — an OSPF stub area "stuck in a route-summarization loop" with
a bad default in Area 4 behind r2 — does not exist in this network. A thorough
inspection of every router's running configuration, OSPF process state, LSA
database, and data-plane forwarding produced no evidence of any anomaly.

Area 4 is correctly configured as a stub on both the ABR (r2) and the single
internal router (r10). The E-bit is set to 0 on all Area 4 LSAs, confirming the
stub flag was negotiated properly between the two peers. r2 self-originates
exactly one default Type-3 summary LSA (0.0.0.0/0, metric 1) into Area 4, and
r10 installs it correctly as O>* 0.0.0.0/0 [110/11] via 21.1.1.1 (r2). r10 also
receives the full set of Type-3 summary LSAs from the backbone, which is expected
behaviour for a regular (non-totally-stubby) stub area.

No area range command exists anywhere in the domain — not on r2, r1, r4, r8, or
any other ABR — so there is no configured inter-area summarization that could
produce a loop. Without a summary range there is no aggregated prefix and no
missing discard route; the classical summarization-loop condition cannot arise.
OSPF neighbors are Full-state on every link, the virtual-link (r4↔r8 through
Area 1) is up, and live pings from r10 (inside the stub) to r9 and r8's Area-2
segment complete with 0% packet loss in both directions.

The engineer's suspicion is a false premise. The network is healthy. No
corrective action is required.
